Subject: jit: export a Linux perf map of JIT'd functions, opt-in via env var
Why: profiles of real gameplay show ~58% of CPU samples landing in anonymous
 JIT mappings with no symbols (docs/PROFILE-doa5.md), which makes flame
 graphs useless for the dominant cost. jit_announce() already receives every
 JIT'd function's address, size and name; a perf-map writer for it exists in
 the tree but is compiled out (#if 0). Re-enable it behind the
 RPCS3_PERF_MAP_DIR environment variable: when set, write
 "<addr> <size> <name>" lines to <dir>/perf-<pid>.map (the perf(1) JIT
 interface format). Re-check the variable while the file is unopened so
 announces from static initializers, which run before the Android bridge can
 setenv() in initialize(), do not permanently disable the export. Keep the
 file on exit (the original deleted it) because on Android symbolization
 happens after the process dies. Also swallow the jit_announce(0,0,"")
 shutdown sentinel instead of logging a spurious "Empty function announced"
 error.
Upstream-status: candidate

